(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).asep=window.HALCYON_DATA.asep||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.asep.full||[]).push({
  num: 4, part: `Part One: Concept`, title: `Shape of the System`, weeks: `Weeks 6 to 9`,
  tasks: [`T4`,`T6`,`M5`,`M6`,`X3`,`X4`,`X5`,`O4`],
  cast: [
    [`Pavel Ruzicka`,`Account lead at Tamarack Life Systems, the supplier building HEARTH's air revitalization rack. Proud of his rack. Busy.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Revision C had come off Wes's plotter three days before the SRR. On Friday of week six Nadia took it to his desk at a quarter past eight, before the board met, before she could talk herself out of it. She'd practiced the first sentence in the hallway. You drew this before anyone decided it.`,
    `Wes's desk was the one by the windows. He had a breakfast burrito going and the structures schedule up on both screens. His paperweight was a test coupon from a pressure vessel, bent in half on purpose.`,
    `She spread the sheet over his keyboard. <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"This is really clean work,"</span> she said.`,
    `So that was what came out instead.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"It's a hatch,"</span> Wes said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"I've drawn forty."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"The aisle's eleven centimeters narrower than the tape."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Seat track down both sides. Crew needs somewhere to clip stuff that isn't the floor."</span> He found note eight with a finger that had salsa on it. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Every cargo module had it."</span>`,
    `Cargo didn't sleep in there. She didn't say that either. She said, <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Can we talk about the aisle?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"We're talking."</span> There was a photo pinned over his desk of the 2019 cargo module in the test cell, the one that leaked at the hatch seal. Wes had found that leak himself. Everybody at FRO knew the story, mostly from Wes. He'd been right about almost everything since she got here, which made this harder.`,
    `Her stomach did its usual thing. The next word was supposed to be no. She was already shopping for a nicer one.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Colleen's Flight Notes: what it does, then where it goes`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Functions first`,
        body: [`Colleen volunteered Saturdays at the air museum at old Lowry, and she met Nadia beside a jet trainer with an access panel off its side. Behind the panel ran a bundle of wire as thick as a wrist, every wire tagged at both ends.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Everybody photographs the cockpit,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"This is the airplane."</span>`,
          `System architecture definition works from the system requirements, and in practice it runs alongside requirements definition, each one shaping the other. Its job is to come up with candidate architectures, pick one or more that best meet the system requirements and the stakeholders' concerns, and describe it in a consistent set of models and views. It takes in the system requirements, the life cycle concepts and scenarios, the constraints, and what's known about the systems around it. It puts out an architecture description: the elements, the interfaces between them, the requirements allocated to each element, and the reasons for the choices. Design definition picks it up next and adds the detail to build or buy each element. System analysis checks the candidates. Integration and verification plan around the interfaces.`,
          `An architecture has two halves. The functional architecture says what the system must do, broken into functions and the flows between them, with no hardware named. Many sources call this the logical architecture. The physical architecture says which elements will do it and how they connect. Allocation joins the two: each function, and each requirement, goes to the element or elements responsible, often with derived requirements written for each. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Wes drew a good airplane,"</span> Colleen said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Now ask him what it's for, one function at a time."</span>`],
        terms: [[`System architecture definition`,`The process that generates architecture alternatives, selects one or more that meet the requirements and frame stakeholder concerns, and expresses them in consistent models and views.`],[`Functional architecture`,`The system's functions and the flows between them, with no hardware named. Also called the logical architecture.`],[`Physical architecture`,`The system's elements and how they connect.`],[`Allocation`,`Assigning functions and requirements to the elements that will meet them.`]],
        exam: `Architecture defines elements and interfaces at the system level. Design definition adds the detail to build them. If an answer jumps to detailed design before functions are allocated, it's out of order.` },
      { h: `Every wire has two ends`,
        body: [`An interface is anywhere two elements exchange something: power, data, heat, fluid, force, or a crew member's hand. Most integration trouble starts at one. An N-squared diagram finds them on purpose. Put the elements on the diagonal. By the usual convention, a mark in an element's row shows what it sends to the element in that column, and a mark in its column shows what it receives. With n elements there are n times one less than n possible one way interfaces, so no pair gets skipped for lack of imagination. Busy rows and columns show the tightly coupled elements. Empty cells are claims too.`,
          `Interface management, as a method, means identifying every interface, defining it, controlling it, and verifying that both sides meet it. A definition says what crosses, in what form, at what values, and who owns each side. It lives in interface requirements and an interface control document, or ICD, that both owners agree to. Once it's agreed, the ICD goes under configuration control, so a change takes a change request, an impact assessment, and sign off from both sides.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Two different crews built the front and back of this airplane,"</span> Colleen said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"The wire works because they agreed on the plug before either one built anything."</span>`],
        terms: [[`N-squared diagram`,`A matrix with the elements on the diagonal and interfaces in the off-diagonal cells: outputs across the row, inputs down the column.`],[`Interface control document (ICD)`,`The agreed, controlled definition of an interface, signed by the owners on both sides.`]],
        exam: `N-squared: row is output, column is input, and n times one less than n possible cells. A change to an agreed ICD is a configuration management item for both parties, not a quick fix by one.` },
      { h: `One model, many views`,
        body: [`Colleen climbed into the front seat and pointed at the gauges. Each one showed one thing to the person who needed it. The architecture description standard, ISO 42010, describes architecture the same way. Stakeholders have concerns. A viewpoint sets the conventions for building a kind of view that frames certain concerns: which models, which notation, which rules. A view applies a viewpoint to this system. The architecture description names the stakeholders and their concerns, and holds the viewpoints, the views, and the reasoning behind them. Helen's crew movement view, the safety office's hazard view, and Tiago's test access view should all come from one model so they can't disagree.`,
          `In model based systems engineering the model is the main work product and documents come out of it. In SysML, a block definition diagram shows what the system is made of, an internal block diagram shows how the parts connect through ports, and activity, sequence and state diagrams show behavior. A digital thread links the data across the life cycle, from requirements to model to CAD, analysis and test, so a change can be followed. A digital twin is a model of one specific built unit kept in step with data from it. HEARTH won't have a twin until there's hardware. Someone also has to decide which copy of the model is the authoritative one, who can read it, and who can change it. That's information management, and on a model based program the model is what it manages.`],
        terms: [[`Viewpoint`,`The conventions for building a kind of view that frames certain stakeholder concerns.`],[`View`,`What you get when you apply a viewpoint to a particular system.`],[`Digital thread`,`Links that connect authoritative data across the life cycle so changes can be traced.`],[`Digital twin`,`A model of one specific physical unit, kept current with data from that unit.`]],
        exam: `Viewpoint is the template; view is the product. Concerns belong to stakeholders. Internal block diagram for ports and connections; block definition diagram for hierarchy.` },
      { h: `One airplane in a big sky`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"This jet was never the system,"</span> Colleen said, and patted the panel closed. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"The system was the jet, the tankers, the radar, the tower, and a thousand people who didn't work for my wing."</span> A system of systems is made of systems that are independent in operation and in management. Each has its own owner, purpose and life cycle, and could run on its own. In a directed one, the whole is built and managed centrally for a set purpose, and the constituents, though they could still run on their own, normally serve that purpose. In an acknowledged one, there are shared goals and a designated manager, but each constituent keeps its own owner and funding. In a collaborative one, the constituents agree to work together with no central authority. A virtual one has neither a central manager nor an agreed central purpose. Halo Station is close to acknowledged: Halo owns the station's rules, and other companies own the modules.`,
          `System analysis supplies the data behind technical decisions. You start from the question, pick a level of detail that fits the decision, write down every assumption, and hand the results, with their uncertainty, to whoever has to decide.`,
          `Before she left, Colleen asked about Jun. INCOSE's systems engineering competency framework sorts what an engineer needs into core, professional, management, technical and integrating groups, at five levels from awareness through supervised practitioner, practitioner and lead practitioner to expert. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Somebody put your name forward once,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Your turn."</span>`],
        terms: [[`System of systems`,`A system whose constituents are themselves systems, independently owned, managed and operated, that together produce results none of them produce alone.`],[`System analysis`,`The process that provides data and information for technical decisions, with stated assumptions and uncertainty.`],[`SE competency framework`,`INCOSE's set of systems engineering competencies and proficiency levels, used to assess and develop engineers.`]],
        exam: `Clues for a system of systems: constituents with their own owners and missions, behavior that only exists across them, and no single owner of everything. Directed, acknowledged, collaborative, virtual runs from most central control to least.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f4s1`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T4`, title:`Revision C`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const a3=all&&all[3];const p=(a3&&a3.picks&&a3.picks[9]!==undefined)?a3.picks[9]:(G.acceptedwesreqs?0:1);
        return [[`The three notes on the drawing matched three lines already in the baseline, lines with no parent and no change request behind them.`,`Wes's three change requests were first on the board's agenda at ten. The drawing had already answered all three.`,`Wes's three rules weren't in the baseline or the board's queue. They were only on the drawing.`,`Wes's three items sat on the board's agenda at ten, deferred from the review with no analysis behind them. The drawing had already decided them.`][p],
        `<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Structures releases the frame drawings in two weeks,"</span> Wes said, <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"or the long lead aluminum slips a month. Amara will tell you the same."</span>`,`Revision C showed where everything went. What does Nadia say?`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Approve revision C as the architecture, and let structure release all nine frames.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-3,health:-5},
          after:`Wes nods and goes back to his burrito. Every subsystem now lays itself out around a drawing, and the aisle is a fact before anyone has asked what the aisle is for.`,
          why:`A drawing of where things go is a physical answer. Architecture starts with what the system must do and then picks elements to do it. Approving the physical layout first makes every function fit around it.`},
        {t:`Hold revision C as a candidate until the functions are mapped, and free safe frames.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`She says it in one breath, which isn't how she rehearsed it. Wes looks at her for a while. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Which frames."</span> She has them circled already. He releases six of nine that afternoon and writes HOLD on the other three in block letters. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Two weeks,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Then I want my aisle back."</span>`,
          why:`Functional and physical architecture develop together, but the functions come first so the elements answer real needs. Treating a drawing as one candidate keeps it honest, and freeing the parts no open function depends on protects the schedule.`},
        {t:`Approve revision C with the aisle width as an owned, dated item for the trade study.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:2,health:1},
          after:`The aisle gets an owner and a date. Everything else on the drawing becomes the architecture by default, and nobody has asked yet what the module has to do.`,
          why:`Tracking the problem you can see is better than losing it. It still treats a physical layout as the architecture before the functions exist.`},
        {t:`Let all nine frames go now, and raise the aisle again at the design review.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The frames release. By the design review there are tooling orders against them, and the aisle has a part number.`,
          why:`Waiting doesn't keep options open. It lets the physical design harden while the functions are still unwritten, and every week makes a change cost more.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s2`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T4`, title:`Verbs, not boxes`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday at noon the alarm she'd set went off, and she made the call she'd promised. Monday she and Jun took the big conference room, the one with the old lighting diagram still ghosted under the whiteboard.`,`Jun had started a function list over the weekend. It was long, and it read like a parts catalog: air rack functions, thermal functions, power functions. <span class="said" data-who="Jun">"That's how Wes splits up the work,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Jun">"So that's how I split up the functions."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep Jun's subsystem lists and add a column for crossing functions.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The crossing column fills up fast. Egress lands in it, and so does every alarm. The lists still assume the subsystems are right, which is the question.`,
          why:`Spotting functions that cross subsystems is good. Starting from subsystems means the functions were allocated before anyone decided they should be.`},
        {t:`Rebuild the list from what HEARTH must do, with no hardware named.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They put five verbs at the top: keep the crew alive, protect them from hazards, house them, connect to the station, support the module for fifteen years. By Wednesday "protect from hazards" has a branch that runs detect fire, warn the crew, stop the air moving, put it out. None of the boxes has a part number. Jun keeps reaching for one and stopping.`,
          why:`A functional architecture describes what the system does and the flows between functions, without naming hardware. Organizing it by subsystem bakes in the physical answer and hides functions no subsystem owns.`},
        {t:`Start from FRO's last cargo module function tree and add a crew branch.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The tree has branches for berthing and cargo transfer. The crew branch looks bolted on, because it is.`,
          why:`A function tree from a system with a different purpose hides what's new. Reuse it as a check, not as the start.`},
        {t:`Skip the function list and go straight to the physical boxes.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`They save a week. Then they can't say which box wakes a sleeping crew member, because no box was ever asked to.`,
          why:`Going straight to physical elements skips the functional view, which is where you find the jobs no single box was given.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s3`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T4`, title:`Sixty four squares`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By Thursday Jun had turned the functions and the subsystems into an N-squared diagram, printed on a plotter sheet that covered the conference table.`,`<prop type="doc" title="HEARTH N-squared diagram, draft 1 (excerpt)">On the diagonal: Structure, Air rack, Thermal, Power, Avionics, Fire detection, Crew systems, Halo Station.<br>Across a row: what that element sends. Down a column: what it receives.<br>Power sends to: air rack, thermal, avionics, fire detection, crew systems.<br>Air rack sends to: thermal (heat), avionics (status), crew systems (cabin air).<br>Thermal sends to: air rack (coolant), avionics (coolant), Halo Station (heat).<br>Halo Station sends to: power (station power), avionics (commands), thermal (radiator loop).<br>Marked cells: 21 of 56.</prop>`,`Tiago leaned on the table with both hands and tapped the air rack's row. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"That one's going to be trouble. Every mess I've ever had in a test cell started in a row like that."</span> Wes wanted to know what anybody planned to do with a chessboard.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Put the matrix in the review package and walk the reviewers through it.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`It's a good picture, and the reviewers like it. Twenty one interfaces still have no owner and no definition.`,
          why:`Showing the matrix is useful. Its value is in what it turns into: a managed list of interfaces.`},
        {t:`Mark only the cells where FRO builds both sides of the link.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The matrix gets tidy. The air rack and Halo Station rows go nearly blank, and those are the two FRO can't fix by walking to the next desk.`,
          why:`Interfaces with suppliers and outside systems are the riskiest, because you don't control the other side. They need managing most.`},
        {t:`Turn each mark into an interface with two owners and a definition.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Twenty one marks become twenty one entries in an interface register, each with two owners and a blank where the definition goes. Only power and thermal have more marks than the air rack, and those two are FRO's own. Tiago circles the air rack's row in pencil and this time doesn't say anything.`,
          why:`An N-squared diagram finds interfaces systematically: each off-diagonal mark is something one element gives another. Turning marks into managed interfaces, and noticing the most coupled elements, is what makes it worth printing.`},
        {t:`Redraw it as a block diagram with arrows for Wes and the reviewers.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The block diagram looks cleaner. Two arrows overlap, and one interface disappears under the legend.`,
          why:`Block diagrams are good for people, and it's easy to leave things off them. The matrix makes you consider every pair.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s4`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X3`, title:`Little squares`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The next week Jun moved the architecture into the model. Wes came by while an internal block diagram was on the screen: a block for each subsystem, small squares along their edges, and lines running between the squares.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"What are the little squares?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Jun">"Ports,"</span> Jun said. <span class="said" data-who="Jun">"Where things go in and out."</span> Wes looked at Nadia as if the answer had been a dare. How should the model show HEARTH's physical architecture?`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep the architecture in slides that Wes can edit, one per subsystem.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Wes edits the slides. Jun edits the model. In two weeks there are two architectures.`,
          why:`Pictures that aren't tied to the model drift. A model gives one source that every view comes from.`},
        {t:`Use block definition diagrams and leave the connections to the ICDs.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The hierarchy is clean. The connections live in documents the model can't check, so the first time two of them disagree, nothing notices.`,
          why:`A block definition diagram shows what the system is made of. Connections and what flows through them belong in the internal block diagram, where the model can check them.`},
        {t:`Model every interface down to its connector pins in the block diagrams now.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Jun spends three nights on pin assignments for connectors that haven't been chosen.`,
          why:`Model to the level the decisions need. Architecture defines elements and interfaces. Pin detail belongs in design.`},
        {t:`Add internal block diagrams with typed ports under the block breakdown.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun types every port: 120 volts DC, coolant at a set flow and temperature, a data link, cabin air. When he joins two ports of different types, the line turns red. He does it once on purpose so Wes can see. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Huh,"</span> Wes says. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"It argues back."</span>`,
          why:`In SysML a block definition diagram shows what the system is made of, and an internal block diagram shows how the parts connect through ports, with typed flows. Typed ports let the model catch a mismatched interface before the hardware does.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s5`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T4`, title:`Where each one lives`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By the end of week seven they were allocating. Each function went to the element that would perform it, and each got a verification point: the place Tiago would prove it worked.`,`Detect fire went to the fire detection and suppression subsystem. Tiago asked where he should verify it. Nadia picked the cabin smoke sensor in the main air return above the galley. Galleys were where fires started. Helen's had. And all of the cabin's air came back there to the fans. Tiago could hold a calibrated smoke source under it and watch it alarm. It was testable, and it would pass. In the verification point column she typed cabin smoke sensor, verified by test, and they moved to the next row.`,`The next row was egress. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Hatch,"</span> Wes said from the doorway. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Hatch is structure. Give it to me."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Allocate egress to structure as Wes asks, with the hatch as its home.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Structure owns egress. The aisle lights and the alarm have no owner, so they have no budget.`,
          why:`Giving a function that needs several elements to just one leaves the other contributors without requirements.`},
        {t:`Allocate egress to every element it needs, with one owner for all of it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`The egress row becomes four rows, each with a derived requirement traced to the parent. Wes gets the hatch and the aisle. Odette gets the alarm, and crew systems gets the lights. Nadia keeps the whole function, and the parent requirement points at all four.`,
          why:`Allocation assigns functions and requirements to elements. When one function needs several elements, each gets a derived requirement traced to the parent, and somebody still owns the function end to end.`},
        {t:`Spread egress across the elements and let each owner write a share.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Each owner writes a share. Added together they don't fit the parent, and nobody owns the gap.`,
          why:`Spreading the function is right. Owners writing their own shares, with no one holding the whole, means the parts don't add up to the need.`},
        {t:`Leave egress unallocated until the trade study settles the layout.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The trade study starts with nothing to compare against, because there's no record of which element does what.`,
          why:`Allocations can be revised. Holding them back means the trade has no baseline to work from.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s6`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T4`, title:`Five pictures`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Halo wanted an architecture walkthrough in week eight. Helen wanted to see how a crew moved through the module. The observer from OCSC wanted the hazard controls. Halo's station integrators wanted interfaces. Tiago wanted to know where his test ports would be.`,`Wes had one answer for all of them. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Send them revision C. It's all on there."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Send revision C and the N-squared to everyone as one picture.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The OCSC observer spends the walkthrough hunting for hazard controls on a structural drawing. Helen asks where the crew is.`,
          why:`One picture rarely answers every stakeholder's concerns. Different concerns need different views.`},
        {t:`Have each lead build slides for their own audience at the walkthrough.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Each deck is good. The crew deck shows the hatch where it was in week six, and the OCSC deck shows it where it is now.`,
          why:`Fitting the picture to the audience is the right idea. Views built by hand, apart from the model, drift apart.`},
        {t:`Build each group a view from the model, framed by its concerns.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Five views, each with its viewpoint written down: crew movement, hazard controls, station interfaces, test access, and a structural view for Wes, which he didn't ask for and reads first. They all come from one model, so when the hatch moves, it moves in all five.`,
          why:`Under ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010, stakeholders have concerns, a viewpoint sets out how to build a view that frames them, and a view applies it to this system. Views drawn from one model stay consistent.`},
        {t:`Give every group read access to the whole model and let them search it.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Helen logs in once and calls Nadia. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"I can't find the crew."</span>`,
          why:`Access isn't a view. Stakeholders need views that answer their concerns, not a whole model to dig through.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s7`, domain:`Agreement and organizational`, task:`O4`, title:`Jun's turn`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Halo's interface working group would meet for the first time at the end of week eight, and Amara wanted FRO's interface list presented. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"You present it,"</span> she told Nadia. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Jun can run the slides."</span>`,`Jun had built every line of that list. He'd presented the trace at the SRR, from notes, to people who'd already read it. He'd never had to answer a customer who hadn't. He was already nodding at Amara.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Do a dry run with Jun and have him present with her beside him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:3},
          after:`They run it twice on Thursday. The second time, Jun stops reading the slides. Afterward they open the competency framework Colleen sent, and Jun picks two to work on, interfaces and communication. He rates himself a supervised practitioner on both. Nadia tells him he's underselling the first one.`,
          why:`Human resource management develops people's competencies on the project, not just assigns them. A supported stretch, prepared and backed up, and a plan against a competency framework grow the person and the program.`},
        {t:`Present the interface list herself at the working group as Amara asked.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`It goes fine. Jun runs slides for a list he wrote and answers one question from the back of the room, after Nadia waves him in.`,
          why:`Protecting the customer meeting is reasonable. Skill grows through real responsibility with support, and it never grows if it's never handed over.`},
        {t:`Let Jun present it on his own, as the person who built the list.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Jun knows the list cold. Then Halo's lead asks who owns the rack coolant interface, and he freezes, because that isn't a fact. It's a negotiation.`,
          why:`Stretch assignments work best with support. Sending someone in alone, unprepared for the hard part, can set him back.`},
        {t:`Present it herself, and ask Amara to send Jun to a modeling course.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Jun gets a training budget and a certificate. He still hasn't spoken in front of the customer.`,
          why:`Training has its place. Competency also grows on the job, and the job was right there.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s8`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X4`, title:`One box of six`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Halo's station integration lead opened the working group with one slide. HEARTH was a small box on it, one of six: the core module, the power and radiator truss, the robotic arm, a lab module another company was building, the visiting crew vehicles, and the ground. Each box had a different owner and a different contract.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"We don't build any of these,"</span> the lead said. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"We own the station."</span> Wes leaned over to Nadia. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Then who's in charge of ours?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Get HEARTH right on its own terms for Halo to fit in later.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`HEARTH's choices are good for HEARTH. Two of them make extra work for the lab module's team, and Halo hears about it from them first.`,
          why:`A constituent optimized alone can make the whole worse. The station's behavior comes from every module's interfaces and operations together.`},
        {t:`Treat HEARTH as one constituent bound by the station's shared rules.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nadia writes it at the top of the interface list: FRO owns HEARTH, Halo owns the station's rules and how the station behaves as a whole, and disagreements go to the working group in writing. The integration lead reads it and asks if Halo can borrow the sentence.`,
          why:`In a system of systems, each constituent keeps its own owner and management. The SoS authority sets the shared rules and owns the behavior that only exists across constituents. Know where your authority ends.`},
        {t:`Follow Halo's interface spec to the letter and decide the rest at FRO.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`HEARTH plugs in cleanly. The spec says nothing about how crews move between modules during an emergency, and so neither does HEARTH.`,
          why:`Meeting the interface spec is necessary. SoS behavior also lives in operations across constituents, which a spec rarely covers completely.`},
        {t:`Ask Halo to take design authority over HEARTH for the station.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Halo declines, politely. They have six contracts and no engineers to spare.`,
          why:`In an acknowledged SoS, constituents keep their own management. Handing over your design authority doesn't make the whole better run.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s9`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X4`, title:`One alarm`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At the start of week nine Halo issued a station change. Every module would use the station's caution and warning tones and the station's panel, so a crew member heard one alarm language wherever they were. HEARTH's own alarm, which Helen had chosen to be heard in every quarter, was out.`,`Odette brought the page to Nadia's desk. <span class="said" data-who="Odette">"One alarm language in every module. That's the right rule,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Odette">"We picked ours so you'd hear it through a curtain, though. I want theirs to do the same."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep HEARTH's own alarm, which Helen chose for every quarter.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`HEARTH keeps a better alarm, and a crew member moving between modules has to learn two. Halo sends FRO a formal letter.`,
          why:`In a system of systems, station-wide rules agreed through the SoS authority govern how the whole behaves. A constituent that opts out makes the whole less safe, even if its own part is better.`},
        {t:`Adopt the station tones as issued and log the audibility question.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`HEARTH matches the station. Whether someone asleep in a bunk hears the new tones sits in a log with no date.`,
          why:`Following the station rule is right. Leaving a known conflict with your own crew requirement open isn't.`},
        {t:`Adopt the station alarm and check it's heard in every bunk.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun traces the change: the alarm function, two interfaces and the audibility requirement. The station tones run a little quieter than HEARTH's. Nadia writes it up, and Halo agrees to a louder speaker in HEARTH inside the station's rules. Helen signs off on the speaker herself.`,
          why:`Station-wide behavior belongs to the SoS authority, so HEARTH follows the shared rule. As a constituent, its job is to assess the impact on its own requirements and settle conflicts formally, not ignore the rule or quietly lose a need.`},
        {t:`Hold the change until Halo issues a paid contract modification.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Amara agrees it's a cost change. The alarm waits on contracts for six weeks, and on paper the crew still has two alarm languages.`,
          why:`Cost belongs in the agreement, worked in parallel. Holding a station safety rule hostage to payment puts the money ahead of the need.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s10`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X3`, title:`The thread`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tiago caught Nadia in the high bay with a draft test procedure in one hand. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"Every number in here I typed from the requirements PDF. If one changes, I won't know. Wes's CAD won't know either. The model knows, and that's it."</span>`,`Jun had ideas about that, and he had them at length. His roommate wrote discharge forecasting software at Halcyon AI, across town, and Jun liked to point out that his own model only had to predict a hatch.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Move requirements, the model, CAD and test procedures into one new tool.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Jun prices the licenses and the migration. It's three months the schedule doesn't have, and Wes says he isn't learning a new CAD tool at fifty eight.`,
          why:`A thread connects tools. It doesn't require replacing them, and a big migration in the middle of a program trades one risk for a bigger one.`},
        {t:`Link requirements, model, CAD and test steps by ID, interfaces first.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun and Tiago wire up the first links in a week. Each test step points to the requirement it checks, and each requirement to the model element and the drawing where it lives. Wes changes a hatch dimension on Thursday. By Friday two of Tiago's test steps are flagged. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"Okay,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"That's worth something."</span>`,
          why:`A digital thread connects authoritative data across the life cycle, so a change can be followed to everything it touches. Starting with the riskiest links gets value early without trying to connect everything at once.`},
        {t:`Export a weekly snapshot of the model for everyone's copies.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`It helps. A requirement that changes on Tuesday is still wrong in Tiago's procedure until Monday.`,
          why:`Regular updates cut the drift. Copies still drift. Links don't.`},
        {t:`Wait for CDR, when the design is stable, to link up all the tools.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`By then there are four hundred requirements, two thousand drawings and nine hundred test steps to connect by hand.`,
          why:`The thread is cheapest while the data is being created. Building it afterward means reconstructing links nobody recorded.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s11`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T6`, title:`Enough cold`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tamarack's proposal gave one number for the heat the air rack would dump into HEARTH's coolant loop. Jun wanted to know whether the loop could take it in every mode the crew would use. Amara wanted the answer by Friday.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Use their number,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"It's in a signed proposal."</span> Tiago, passing with a cart, said thermal vacuum testing would settle it for sure in about two years.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Check Tamarack's proposal number against the loop's full capacity.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`There's margin. What the page doesn't say is that the number is the rack's average, and the crew's exercise hours aren't average.`,
          why:`One number from another party is an assumption, not a result. Analysis states its assumptions and checks the cases that drive the decision.`},
        {t:`Order a full computer model of the cabin's heat and airflow first.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`It'll take six weeks and a contractor. The answer they need is a sum by mode, and Jun could have it by Friday.`,
          why:`More fidelity isn't automatically better analysis. Match it to the question and the decision it feeds.`},
        {t:`Run a heat balance mode by mode, writing down each assumption.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun builds a heat balance for each mode in two days: sleep, nominal, exercise, and a hot orbit. The loop holds in all of them except exercise in a hot orbit, where it's tight. At the top of the page he writes: rack heat from Tamarack proposal, not design data. Nadia circles that line.`,
          why:`System analysis gives decision makers data they can trust: the right question, detail matched to the decision, assumptions and uncertainty stated. Its results feed architecture and design.`},
        {t:`Wait for thermal vacuum testing to measure the real rack.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Two years is a long time not to know whether the loop is big enough, and the loop's size gets frozen in five months.`,
          why:`Test confirms. Analysis is how you decide before the hardware exists.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s12`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X3`, title:`Drop right in`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Halo sent its interface requirements for the berthing mechanism, station power, data and the radiator loop. The same afternoon Pavel Ruzicka at Tamarack sent an email. Their rack came from the same family they'd flown on the old station, he wrote. It would drop right in, and the details could be sorted out at integration, the way they always had been.`,`Jun's list had twenty one interfaces. Eleven had a definition both sides had signed.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Run Halo's interfaces through ICDs and settle Tamarack's on email with Pavel.`, s:0, flag:`nointerfacecontrol`, d:{trust:3,conf:-3,health:-6},
          after:`The station interfaces get ICDs and signatures. The rack gets a friendly email thread with Pavel, and three of its interfaces have no owner on either side.`,
          why:`An interface inside your own supply chain is still an interface. Handling it informally is how two sides build to two different ideas of one connector.`},
        {t:`Use ICDs for the station and a Tamarack datasheet for the rack.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The datasheet arrives. It describes the rack, not the agreement between the rack and HEARTH, and nobody signs it.`,
          why:`A datasheet is data, not an agreement. ICDs are agreed and controlled by both sides.`},
        {t:`Let Tamarack define the rack's interfaces and fit HEARTH to them.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`HEARTH starts designing to a rack whose next version Tamarack hasn't mentioned yet.`,
          why:`Adopting a supplier's proven design can be a good trade if it's decided on purpose. Handing over the interface definition with no agreement leaves nothing to hold either side to.`},
        {t:`Bring the rack's interfaces under signed ICDs, the same as the station's.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Halo's integration lead takes the station side. Pavel agrees, sounding a little hurt, to send Tamarack's interface data for an ICD by the end of the week. Every row on the list has two owners and a due date.`,
          why:`Interface management identifies, defines, controls and verifies interfaces, with both sides agreeing to an ICD that goes under configuration control. Supplier interfaces need it as much as outside ones.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f4s3`, title:`Counting the squares`, setup:[`Jun's N-squared diagram has 8 elements on the diagonal. Each off-diagonal cell is one possible interface in one direction: what the row element sends to the column element. Jun marked 21 cells.`,`Possible one way interfaces equal n times one less than n. Percent equals the count divided by the total, times 100. Wes wants the hatch and berthing mechanism split out of structure as a ninth element.`],
      fields:[{label:`Possible one way interfaces with 8 elements`, answer:56, tol:0},{label:`Percent of cells marked`, answer:37.5, tol:0.01},{label:`Possible one way interfaces with 9 elements`, answer:72, tol:0},{label:`New cells added by the ninth element`, answer:16, tol:0}],
      solution:[`8 times 7 = 56 possible one way interfaces.`,`21 / 56 = 0.375, or 37.5 percent.`,`With 9 elements: 9 times 8 = 72.`,`72 minus 56 = 16 new cells: 8 in the new element's row and 8 in its column.`,`The marked cells aren't the only ones that matter. Every empty cell says two elements don't talk to each other, and that's a claim somebody should check.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f4s5`, title:`Which element does it?`, intro:`Allocation gives each function to the element that performs it. Pick the HEARTH subsystem each function goes to.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Remove carbon dioxide from the cabin air.`, choices:[`Thermal control`,`Air revitalization rack`,`Crew systems`,`Power distribution`], answer:1, why:`CO2 removal is a life support job, and in HEARTH that's the air revitalization rack.`},
      {prompt:`Carry heat from the equipment to the station's radiators.`, choices:[`Structure`,`Air revitalization rack`,`Thermal control`,`MMOD shielding`], answer:2, why:`Collecting heat and rejecting it to the host's radiators is active thermal control.`},
      {prompt:`Take station power and deliver it to HEARTH's loads.`, choices:[`Power distribution`,`Avionics and data`,`Thermal control`,`Crew systems`], answer:0, why:`HEARTH has no power source of its own. Power distribution takes the host's power and routes it to each load.`},
      {prompt:`Carry commands and telemetry between HEARTH and the station.`, choices:[`Power distribution`,`Crew systems`,`Fire detection and suppression`,`Avionics and data`], answer:3, why:`Commands in and telemetry out ride the data system.`},
      {prompt:`Hold cabin pressure and carry launch loads.`, choices:[`Structure and mechanisms`,`MMOD shielding`,`Thermal control`,`Air revitalization rack`], answer:0, why:`The pressure shell and its frames are primary structure.`},
      {prompt:`Stop micrometeoroids and orbital debris before they reach the pressure shell.`, choices:[`Structure and mechanisms`,`Thermal control`,`MMOD shielding`,`Avionics and data`], answer:2, why:`That's the job of the MMOD shielding outside the shell.`},
      {prompt:`Give each crew member a private place to sleep.`, choices:[`Air revitalization rack`,`Crew systems`,`Structure and mechanisms`,`Power distribution`], answer:1, why:`Crew quarters, the galley and the hygiene station are crew systems.`},
      {prompt:`Warn the crew. In HEARTH this one goes to more than one element. Which of these is one of them?`, choices:[`MMOD shielding`,`Thermal control`,`Structure and mechanisms`,`Avionics and data`], answer:3, why:`Warnings travel as data to the panel and speakers, so avionics and data carries part of it. When a function lands on several elements, each gets a derived requirement traced to the parent.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`What is the main purpose of the system architecture definition process?`, opts:[`Detail each element enough to build, buy or code it`,`Create and select an architecture that meets requirements`,`Turn stakeholder needs into verifiable requirements`,`Assemble elements into the whole and check interfaces`], a:1, why:`Architecture definition generates alternatives, selects one or more that frame stakeholder concerns and meets the system requirements, and expresses it in consistent views. Detail for building is design definition.`, task:`T4`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`A diagram shows what a system must do and the flows between those tasks, with no hardware named. What is it?`, opts:[`A physical architecture`,`An interface control document`,`A functional architecture`,`A block definition diagram`], a:2, why:`A functional architecture describes functions and their flows without naming the elements that perform them. A physical architecture names the elements.`, task:`T4`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`In an N-squared diagram drawn with the usual convention, what does a mark in an off-diagonal cell show?`, opts:[`An output from the row element to the column element`,`A requirement allocated to both of those elements`,`A function that neither element performs on its own`,`A test that has to verify both elements together`], a:0, why:`Elements sit on the diagonal. Reading across a row shows what that element sends, and reading down a column shows what it receives. Each mark is an interface.`, task:`T4`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`An engineer gives "warn the crew" to the alarm panel, the lights and the cabin speakers, and writes a derived requirement for each. What is this?`, opts:[`Functional decomposition`,`Interface definition`,`Requirements validation`,`Allocation to elements`], a:3, why:`Allocation assigns functions and requirements to the elements that will meet them. When one function lands on several elements, each gets a derived requirement traced to the parent.`, task:`T4`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Under ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010, what does an architecture viewpoint do?`, opts:[`It lists every element of the system and its interfaces`,`It sets conventions for views that frame certain concerns`,`It records one stakeholder's approval of the architecture`,`It describes the system from the physical side only`], a:1, why:`A viewpoint sets the conventions, such as models, notation and rules, for building a kind of view that frames particular stakeholder concerns. A view applies it to a specific system.`, task:`T4`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Which process takes the selected architecture and its allocated requirements next, and adds the detail needed to build or buy each element?`, opts:[`System requirements definition`,`Integration`,`Design definition`,`Implementation`], a:2, why:`Design definition provides the detailed data for each element, consistent with the architecture. Implementation then builds or buys it.`, task:`T4`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Which SysML diagram best shows how the parts of a block connect to each other through ports?`, opts:[`Internal block diagram`,`Block definition diagram`,`Requirement diagram`,`Activity diagram`], a:0, why:`An internal block diagram shows a block's parts and the connectors between their ports. A block definition diagram shows the hierarchy of what the system is made of.`, task:`X3`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`What does a digital thread provide on a program?`, opts:[`A virtual copy of one built unit, kept in sync`,`One tool that replaces CAD, requirements and test`,`A weekly export of the model to every team`,`Links that connect data across the life cycle`], a:3, why:`A digital thread connects authoritative data across the life cycle so a change can be followed to everything it affects. A virtual copy of one built unit is a digital twin.`, task:`X3`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`A supplier says its unit will "drop right in" and the interfaces can be sorted out at integration. What does interface management call for instead?`, opts:[`Accept the supplier's design and adapt the system to it`,`Agree an ICD with both owners and control it from then on`,`Check the supplier's datasheet when the unit is delivered`,`Let integration resolve mismatches as they come up`], a:1, why:`Interface management identifies, defines, controls and verifies interfaces. Both owners agree an ICD early, and it goes under configuration control.`, task:`X3`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`Which feature most clearly marks a system of systems?`, opts:[`Its parts are all built to one shared set of drawings`,`It has more than a thousand separately made parts`,`Its constituents are independently owned and run`,`It is managed as one program by a single office`], a:2, why:`The constituents of a system of systems are operationally and managerially independent. Size alone doesn't make one, and one owner of everything makes it an ordinary system.`, task:`X4`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`A station operator sets station-wide goals and rules, but each module keeps its own owner, funding and contract. Which type of system of systems is this closest to?`, opts:[`Acknowledged`,`Directed`,`Collaborative`,`Virtual`], a:0, why:`An acknowledged SoS has recognized shared objectives and a designated manager, while the constituents keep their own ownership and funding. Directed would mean central control of the constituents.`, task:`X4`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`A team must decide whether a coolant loop can take a rack's heat in each operating mode. Which approach best fits system analysis?`, opts:[`Compare the supplier's single heat value to capacity`,`Commission the highest fidelity model available first`,`Wait for thermal vacuum testing on the real hardware`,`Fit the detail to the decision and state assumptions`], a:3, why:`System analysis provides data for decisions, with fidelity matched to the question and assumptions and uncertainty stated. A single borrowed number is an assumption, and waiting for test isn't analysis.`, task:`T6`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`What is INCOSE's systems engineering competency framework mainly used for?`, opts:[`Certifying that a system meets all its requirements`,`Assessing and developing engineers' competencies`,`Ranking suppliers by their engineering maturity`,`Defining which life cycle processes a project uses`], a:1, why:`The framework sets out systems engineering competencies and proficiency levels from awareness to expert, so people and organizations can assess and plan development.`, task:`X5`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`Two parties have signed an interface control document. One now needs to change a connector type. What should happen?`, opts:[`A change request, assessed and approved by both sides`,`The changing side updates its drawings and tells the other`,`The change waits until integration shows if it matters`,`The customer decides alone, since it owns the system`], a:0, why:`An agreed ICD is under configuration control. A change needs a change request, an impact assessment, and approval from the owners on both sides.`, task:`M5`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`Which process makes sure each stakeholder gets the right access to current model information, with clear rules on who can read and who can change it?`, opts:[`Configuration management`,`Knowledge management`,`Quality assurance`,`Information management`], a:3, why:`Information management gets the right information to the right people, current and protected, with access rules and records. Configuration management controls changes to baselined items.`, task:`M6`, domain:`Technical management`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{G=G||{};const noIC=(s.flags&&s.flags.nointerfacecontrol)||G.nointerfacecontrol;const pj=(s.picks&&s.picks[6]!==undefined)?s.picks[6]:0;
    return [
    [`At the end of week eight Jun had presented FRO's interface list to Halo's working group, with Nadia in the chair beside him. When Halo's lead asked who owned the rack coolant interface, he answered without looking at her.`,`At the end of week eight Nadia had presented FRO's interface list to Halo's working group. Jun ran the slides and took one question at the end.`,`At the end of week eight Jun had presented FRO's interface list to Halo's working group by himself. He still didn't want to talk about the coolant question.`,`At the end of week eight Nadia had presented FRO's interface list to Halo's working group. Jun ran the slides, with his course enrollment confirmed in his inbox.`][pj],
    `On Friday Tamarack's interface data arrived.`,
    (noIC
      ?`It came as an attachment to one of Pavel's friendly emails, forwarded twice. FRO had never asked Tamarack for it. Jun found it while he was looking for something else, in a thread Wes had been copied on, and loaded it into the model anyway.`
      :`It came in on the date Jun had asked for, with Pavel's name at the bottom. Jun loaded it into the model that afternoon.`),
    `He gave the rack a block in the model, typed its ports from Pavel's data, and connected them to HEARTH's. Three of the four lines turned red.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="HEARTH model, interface check: Tamarack air revitalization rack">Power: rack input 28 V DC. HEARTH distribution 120 V DC. MISMATCH.<br>Coolant: rack quick disconnects one size smaller than HEARTH's, on the opposite face of the rack. MISMATCH.<br>Data: rack on a fifteen fifty three bus. HEARTH avionics on Ethernet. MISMATCH.<br>Structure: rack mounting points. MATCH.</prop>`,
    `Jun looked at the screen and then at her. <span class="said" data-who="Jun">"It doesn't plug in,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Jun">"Not one of the three."</span>`,
    (s.score>=28
      ?`Wes read the three red lines standing up and didn't say drop right in, though she could see him think it. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"When you call Pavel,"</span> he said, <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"I want to be on the line. I've plugged his racks into things before."</span>`
      :`Wes read the three red lines and said Tamarack had always been good people. He went back to structures without his coffee.`),
    `Nadia went to the whiteboard. The 133 was still there in its red box. Under it she wrote RACK: POWER, COOLANT, DATA.`,
    `She opened a new email to Pavel and wrote the first line before she could think of a kinder one.`,
    `Pavel, it doesn't plug in.`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch04.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Trade`
});
